# Deny-by-default allowlist, not a denylist: the Dockerfile's COPY
# instructions only ever name pyproject.toml, uv.lock, README.md,
# alembic.ini, and src/ explicitly, so nothing else the build context
# contains should even be *sendable* to the daemon -- not tests/, not
# docs/ (which holds the PRD and this milestone's own plan), not .git/,
# not a real .env with real secrets, not any of the local tool caches.
# Docker uploads the whole context before evaluating COPY, regardless of
# what COPY actually references, so this is what keeps that upload small
# and keeps anything unlisted from ever being one stray `COPY . .` away
# from landing in an image layer.
*

!pyproject.toml
!uv.lock
!README.md
!alembic.ini
!src
!src/**

# Re-exclude, evaluated after the allowlist above: .dockerignore patterns
# are last-match-wins, so these still win against `!src/**`. Without them,
# verified directly, host-local __pycache__/*.pyc under src/ (created by
# any local `uv run pytest`/mypy invocation, gitignored but *not*
# dockerignored by the allowlist alone) leaked into the built image --
# 11 stale __pycache__ dirs, compiled against whatever CPython happened
# to be on this host, copied in via COPY src/ ./src/. Harmless to running
# the app (uv sync's own UV_COMPILE_BYTECODE=1 regenerates real bytecode
# for the installed venv separately), but it defeats the point of an
# allowlist -- two developers building the same commit could ship
# different image contents depending on what cruft their working tree
# happened to have lying around.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
